.TH GP-ACCOUNT 1 "2026-08-13" "gitplus" "gitplus Manual"
.SH NAME
gp-account \- tie directories to different git/ssh/GitHub identities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gp account add
.I NAME
.BI \-\-email " EMAIL"
.RI [ \-\-name " GIT-NAME"]
.RI [ \-\-host " HOST"]
.RI [ \-\-dir " PATH"]
.RI [ \-\-gh\-user " USER"]
.br
.B gp account bind
.I NAME DIR
.br
.B gp account list
.br
.B gp account key
.I NAME
.br
.B gp account check
.SH DESCRIPTION
Work split across more than one GitHub account \(em a personal one and an
employer's \(em normally means remembering to set
.I user.email
per clone and juggling ssh keys by hand.
.B gp account
sets an account up once, and every repository under a bound directory then
commits as it, reaches the host with its key, and (with
.BR \-\-gh\-user )
has
.B gh
talk to GitHub as it.
.PP
Nothing is sourced into your shell. Git reads
.I includeIf
from your git config and ssh reads the
.I Host
alias from your ssh config on their own, so this is persistent configuration
plus a keypair. Edits are always APPENDED, wrapped in
.I BEGIN/END
sentinel comments, and never rewritten in place \(em a botched rewrite of an
ssh config can lock you out of every host you use. An existing key, host
block or identity file is left alone and reported rather than clobbered.
.PP
The other git-* commands use this: each one resolves the account bound to the
current directory and scopes its
.B gh
calls to that identity, so the right account is used no matter which shell,
script or agent invoked it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-email " EMAIL"
Required. The git
.I user.email
for the account.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " GIT-NAME"
The git
.IR user.name .
Defaults to
.IR NAME .
.TP
.BI \-\-host " HOST"
The git host. Defaults to
.IR github.com .
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " PATH"
Bind the account to
.I PATH
in the same step, equivalent to a following
.BR "gp account bind" .
.TP
.BI \-\-gh\-user " USER"
Pair the account with a
.B gh
CLI login. This is what lets the other commands, and the optional
.I ghswitch
shell hook, pick the right GitHub identity per directory.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B check
The one worth running. Verifies every account and binding still applies and
exits non-zero if not. This matters because the main failure mode is silent:
rename or move a bound directory and its
.I includeIf
points at a path that no longer exists, so git quietly falls back to your
global identity and the wrong name lands on every commit with no error
anywhere. Where a repository exists under a binding, it confirms end to end
that git really resolves the expected address.
.TP
.B key
Print the account's public key again \(em for pasting into the host, or for
authorising it against an SSO-enforcing organisation.
.SH SHELL HOOK
.I share/zsh/ghswitch.zsh
is optional and keeps
.BR gh 's
identity matched to the directory you are in, per shell, by exporting
.B GH_TOKEN
rather than changing
.BR gh 's
machine-wide active account. Source it from your
.IR ~/.zshrc .
Without it the commands still resolve the right account themselves; the hook
only helps bare
.B gh
invocations you type yourself.
.SH HISTORY
This was
.B devrig account
until gitplus grew a hard dependency on it. Configuration written by that
version is still read: the sentinel comments it wrote used a
.I devrig:
prefix, and both that and the current
.I gitplus:
prefix are understood, so existing setups keep working with no migration. New
entries are written with the current prefix, and a file containing a mix of
both is fine.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GITPLUS_SSH_CONFIG
Override the ssh config written to (default
.IR ~/.ssh/config ).
.TP
.B GITPLUS_GITCONFIG
Override the git config written to (default
.IR ~/.gitconfig ).
.TP
.B GITPLUS_SSH_KEY_DIR
Override where keypairs are generated (default
.IR ~/.ssh ).
.PP
The
.I DEVRIG_*
and
.I EZENV_*
names this command used previously are still honoured as fallbacks.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.gitconfig-<account>
Per-account git identity, included from your git config for bound directories.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_<account>
Per-account ssh key. Generated without a passphrase \(em this is a convenience
tool, and a passphrase would need agent plumbing on every clone. Protected by
file permissions only.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
.RS
gp account add work --email me@work.com --dir ~/code/work --gh-user work-gh
# paste the printed key into that host account, then:
git clone git@github.com-work:acme/api.git ~/code/work/api
gp account check
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gp-pr (1),
.BR gh (1)
